End open address ranges between files edited with -i.
With -i each file is edited on its own: line numbers and '$' restart in
every file, and the loop between files restores line addresses that a
match had disabled. But a range still open at the end of a file stayed
open, so it went on through the next file, and the ones after it, until
its end address matched. "sed -i '/START/,/END/d' a b", with a START
but no END in a, deleted b from its first line up to END, and
"sed -i 2,5d a b", with three lines in each, emptied b. Close every
range between files too, as GNU sed does with -i and -s.

---
 editors/sed.c |    4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -1691,10 +1691,12 @@
 			free(G.outname);
 			G.outname = NULL;
 
-			/* Fix disabled range matches and mangled ",+N" ranges */
+			/* Fix disabled range matches and mangled ",+N" ranges,
+			 * and end ranges left open: each file is separate */
 			for (sed_cmd = G.sed_cmd_head; sed_cmd; sed_cmd = sed_cmd->next) {
 				sed_cmd->beg_line = sed_cmd->beg_line_orig;
 				sed_cmd->end_line = sed_cmd->end_line_orig;
+				sed_cmd->in_match = 0;
 			}
 		}
 		/* Here, to handle "sed 'cmds' nonexistent_file" case we did:
